::ENG4_U03_L07_GP_Q01::[MLA.ENG4.R.12] Passage: A county memo recommends weekend cooling centers because heat-related emergency calls rose during extreme heat warnings. It uses a problem-solution structure, cites call data, and calls the plan a "limited pilot." Which evaluation is strongest?
{
~A. The memo is weak because it is not emotional.#Incorrect
=B. The memo is effective because its structure, evidence, and cautious rhetoric fit county decision-makers.#Correct
~C. The memo is effective only because it includes the word pilot.#Incorrect
~D. The memo has no argument because it is a memo.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_L07_GP_Q02::[MLA.ENG4.R.05] Passage: The text first describes a documented shortage, then proposes a temporary service, then explains how results will be measured. What structure is used?
{
=A. Problem-solution with implementation review.#Correct
~B. Chronological biography.#Incorrect
~C. Literary flashback.#Incorrect
~D. Unrelated list.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_L07_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG4.R.06] Passage: A proposal claims that evening computer lab hours will help adults complete job applications. It cites application deadlines, current lab availability, and resident requests for evening access. How effective is the support?
{
~A. Weak, because all support must be emotional.#Incorrect
~B. Weak, because job applications are unrelated to computer labs.#Incorrect
=C. Strong, because the support connects the need, resource, and audience problem.#Correct
~D. Strong only because there are three details.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_L07_GP_Q04::[MLA.ENG4.R.08] Passage A supports a program with cost data. Passage B supports the same program with a resident story. Which evaluation is most precise?
{
~A. Passage A is always better because numbers are always best.#Incorrect
~B. Passage B is always better because stories are memorable.#Incorrect
~C. Both passages fail because they use different support.#Incorrect
=D. Passage A may fit decision-makers, while Passage B may build public concern.#Correct
}
::ENG4_U03_L07_GP_Q05::[MLA.ENG4.C.01] A student must briefly present an evaluation of a policy argument. Which opening is strongest?
{
~A. I liked the topic, so the argument was good.#Incorrect
=B. The argument is effective for city leaders because its evidence, structure, and cautious tone make the proposal practical.#Correct
~C. The passage had some sentences about a problem.#Incorrect
~D. I do not know much about this issue.#Incorrect
}
